---
.title = "Bluetooth",
.description = "Bluetooth LE advertising: a beacon nearby devices can see, from Zig or the page, with what each platform's stack can send.",
.date = .date("2026-10-14T00:00:00"),
.authors = ["Oriel"],
.layout = "docs.shtml",
---

`oriel.bluetooth` makes the device **advertise**: it sends a small Bluetooth
LE beacon (service UUIDs, service data, the device's name) that phones and
computers nearby see when they scan. Apps use it to say "I'm here" before
anything connects, as Quick Share's wake-up beacon (service data under
`0xFE2C`) does.

The same API builds on every platform. What each platform's Bluetooth stack
can put in an advertisement differs, and `capabilities()` says so: a field
the device can't send is `error.Unsupported`, never silently dropped.

# [Availability]($heading.id('availability'))

| Platform | Backend | Service UUIDs | Service data | Device name | Connectable | Non-connectable |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Android, ChromeOS | `BluetoothLeAdvertiser` | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Linux | BlueZ (`org.bluez.LEAdvertisement1`, system bus) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| macOS, iOS | `CBPeripheralManager` | ✓ | — | ✓ | ✓ | best-effort |
| Windows | `BluetoothLEAdvertisementPublisher` | ✓ | ✓ | — | — | ✓ |

- **Apple** can't advertise service data, and its advertisements are always
  connectable: `connectable = false` is accepted, nothing is published to
  connect to, and `capabilities().non_connectable` is false. So Quick
  Share's `0xFE2C` beacon can't be sent from macOS or iOS; check
  `capabilities().service_data` and hide that feature there.
- **Windows** rejects the device name in advertisements, and its legacy
  advertisements can't be connected to.
- **One at a time** on Apple, and on Android adapters without
  multiple-advertisement support (ChromeOS's ARC): a second `advertise`
  while one runs is `error.Failed`.
- Advertisements are **legacy** (31 bytes of payload). A connectable one
  spends 3 of them on the Flags structure, and the name counts too.

The module is the `bluetooth` flag, **off by default**. Declaring
`.permissions.bluetooth` in `addApp` turns it on and writes each platform's
declarations: Android's `BLUETOOTH_ADVERTISE`, `BLUETOOTH_SCAN` and
`BLUETOOTH_CONNECT` (and the pre-12 `BLUETOOTH`/`BLUETOOTH_ADMIN`), iOS's
and macOS's `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription` with your text. With the
module on, the page sees `window.oriel.features.bluetooth == true`.

# [Quick start]($heading.id('quick-start'))

```zig
// build.zig
_ = oriel.addApp(b, dep, .{
    .name = "beacon",
    .root_source_file = b.path("src/main.zig"),
    .frontend = .{ .static_dir = b.path("web") },
    .permissions = .{ .bluetooth = "Let nearby phones find this device" },
});
```

```zig
// Zig: ask first (Android 12+, iOS, macOS prompt), then advertise.
const bt = oriel.bluetooth;
if (bt.capabilities().service_data) {
    const h = try bt.advertise(.{ .service_data = .{ .uuid16 = 0xFE2C, .data = payload } });
    defer bt.stop(h);
    // ...
}
```

```js
// The page
const caps = await oriel.bluetooth.capabilities();
oriel.listen("bluetooth:advertise", (e) => console.log(e.id, e.state, e.error));
const ad = await oriel.bluetooth.advertise({ serviceUuids16: [0x180F], localName: true });
// ...
await oriel.bluetooth.stop(ad);
```

# [Advertisements]($heading.id('advertisements'))

| Zig (`Advertisement`) | Page | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `service_uuids16` | `serviceUuids16` | 16-bit service UUIDs to list |
| `service_data` | `serviceData: { uuid16, data }` | Bytes under a 16-bit service UUID (`data`: a `Uint8Array` in the page) |
| `local_name` | `localName` | Include the system's device name |
| `connectable` | `connectable` | Accept connections (default false) |
| `mode` | `mode` | `low_latency`, `balanced` (default) or `low_power` |
| `tx_power` | `txPower` | `high`, `medium` (default), `low` or `ultra_low` |

`advertise` returns a handle (the page gets `{ id }`), and `stop` ends it.
An advertisement with nothing in it is `error.Unsupported`.

# [Errors and state]($heading.id('errors-and-state'))

`advertise` fails right away with:

| Error | When |
|---|---|
| `Unsupported` | No BLE peripheral role here, or a field this device can't send |
| `PermissionDenied` | The bluetooth permission isn't granted (`oriel.permissions.request(.bluetooth)`) |
| `AdapterOff` | Bluetooth is off |
| `TooLarge` | It doesn't fit the 31-byte payload |
| `Failed` | The platform refused it (or one is already running where only one can) |

Most stacks answer the start later, so a returned handle isn't a beacon in
the air yet. The answer comes as the advertisement's state: `started`,
`failed` (with the error) or `stopped` (the platform ended it, e.g. the
adapter turned off). Zig sets `oriel.bluetooth.onAdvertiseState(handler)`;
the page listens to `bluetooth:advertise`, `{ id, state, error }`.

`capabilities()` also tells `powered` (the adapter is on), `extended` and
`max_bytes`; the permission is separate, `oriel.permissions.status(.bluetooth)`.
`oriel check` prints them.

